            <ArticleTitle>Relationship between benign prostatic hyperplasia and history of coronary artery disease in elderly men.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>383-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the relationship between the occurrence of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), an androgen-dependent disease, and coronary artery disease (defined as history of coronary artery bypass grafting, coronary angioplasty, myocardial infarction) in elderly men.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective chart review.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Urology practice.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Seven hundred two elderly men aged 65-80 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">The men's charts were reviewed for data pertaining to coronary artery disease, risk factors for coronary artery disease, and serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels. Men who had medical conditions, pharmacologic interventions, or surgical procedures that could alter PSA, and those taking lipid-lowering agents were not included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">PSA levels correlate positively with prostatic volume of BPH. In men with levels under 1.0 pg/L (no BPH) and over 1.0 microg/L (BPH present), the frequency of coronary artery disease was 9% and 29%, respectively (p&lt;0.03). No significant differences were noted between groups in other accepted risk factors for coronary artery disease including age, smoking, diabetes mellitus, or hypertension.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Smooth muscle proliferation is an important and possibly androgen-dependent step in the development of atherosclerosis and BPH. Prospective studies are required to assess the effect of antiandrogens on atherosclerosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
